# PART 7 — AI-ASSISTED SAFETY RISK ASSESSMENT

This is one of the central intelligent features of CYBER WOMEN.

Build an analytical system that studies historical incident patterns across geographical locations and time periods.

The objective is to identify areas and time windows where historical reports show relatively higher concentrations of incidents.

Example:

An area has repeatedly recorded incidents between 8 PM and 11 PM.

The system should provide an insight such as:

"Historical reports indicate a higher concentration of reported incidents in this area between 8 PM and 11 PM. Consider using a well-lit alternative route and informing a trusted contact."

This is an aggregate historical risk assessment.

It is NOT a guaranteed prediction of future crime.

## 7.1 Data dimensions

Analyse:

- Geographic location.
- Historical incident frequency.
- Time of day.
- Day of week.
- Incident category.
- Reporting frequency.
- Data recency.
- Data completeness.
- Geographic concentration.
- Historical trends.

## 7.2 Analytical methods

Where appropriate, implement:

- Time-series analysis.
- Geographic clustering.
- Density analysis.
- Historical frequency comparison.
- Time-of-day analysis.
- Day-of-week analysis.
- Statistical anomaly detection.

## 7.3 Risk assessment outputs

Display:

- Relative risk category.
- Geographic area.
- Relevant time window.
- Supporting historical data.
- Confidence or reliability indicator.
- Data sufficiency status.
- Recommended precautions.
- Date of last data update.

Do not manufacture confidence scores.

If data is insufficient, show:

"Insufficient verified historical data to calculate a reliable risk assessment."

## 7.4 Data transparency

Clearly classify:

1. Verified historical reports.
2. Unverified user reports.
3. Synthetic demonstration records.
4. AI-generated insights.

Never mix synthetic records into real-world statistics without an explicitly labelled simulation mode.

Never describe an AI-generated insight as an official police warning.

Never claim AI can predict individual crimes.

