The SOS interface must be easy to use.

Avoid unnecessary steps.

Provide an appropriate cancellation mechanism to reduce accidental activations.

## 8.2 SOS workflow

When SOS is activated:

1. Generate a unique incident ID.
2. Record the activation timestamp.
3. Obtain the latest permitted location.
4. Record location accuracy.
5. Identify configured trusted contacts.
6. Generate an emergency message.
7. Submit the event to the secure backend.
8. Send alerts through configured providers.
9. Track provider responses.
10. Display the current emergency status.
11. Preserve the incident in emergency history.

Example message:

"EMERGENCY ALERT: I need assistance. My current location is [location]. Please contact me immediately. Emergency reference: [incident ID]."

## 8.3 Emergency status

Implement:

- Created.
- Queued.
- Sent.
- Delivered.
- Acknowledged.
- Assistance in progress.
- Resolved.
- Failed.
- Cancelled.

Only display Delivered when a provider confirms delivery.

Only display Acknowledged when a recipient has actually acknowledged the alert.

Never fabricate a successful alert.

## 8.4 Trusted contacts

Users must be able to:

- Add contacts.
